So, this is the reason why there's an equal opportunity

466
00:30:59,260 --> 00:31:06,260
because the market is accepting fair value, buyers and sellers are agreeing to a price.

467
00:31:06,260 --> 00:31:12,260
And by that token, neither buyers want to buy premium nor do the sellers want to sell discount prices.

468
00:31:12,260 --> 00:31:18,260
And for that reason, there's an equal opportunity for the market to go up or go down.

469
00:31:18,260 --> 00:31:24,260
Then, the opposite of that is non-symmetrical profiles or not balanced.

470
00:31:24,260 --> 00:31:26,441
Now, as you can see in the pictures,

471
00:31:26,441 --> 00:31:28,350
our first blue little picture there, that's

472
00:31:28,350 --> 00:31:30,259
what a typical normal distribution looks like.

473
00:31:30,260 --> 00:31:34,974
What you'll notice is that the majority of the trade is executed

474
00:31:34,974 --> 00:31:40,474
within 70% to 90% of the mean. In other words, the average price on the day.

475
00:31:41,260 --> 00:31:43,500
Now, non-symmetrical profiles, as you can see

476
00:31:43,500 --> 00:31:45,740
by the pictures, have somewhat of a

477
00:31:45,740 --> 00:31:49,260
skew. It could be a positive skew or a negative skew.

478
00:31:49,260 --> 00:31:56,260
Now, non-symmetrical profiles, by that very definition, mean non-equal opportunities.

479
00:31:56,260 --> 00:31:59,738
Now, it's important you understand the idea of non-symmetry because

480
00:31:59,738 --> 00:32:04,259
it's non-symmetry that starts to give us a little bit of a lean,

481
00:32:04,260 --> 00:32:10,260
not necessarily a bias, but a little bit of a lean in one direction or the other.

482
00:32:10,260 --> 00:32:11,974
In other words, imagine I said

483
00:32:11,974 --> 00:32:14,545
to you you walked into a casino and I

484
00:32:14,545 --> 00:32:17,116
said to you there's roughly a 60% chance that we

485
00:32:17,116 --> 00:32:19,973
could land on the color red on the roulette table.

486
00:32:20,260 --> 00:32:25,260
I think most of you would agree you would look to take that opportunity as you have an edge.

487
00:32:25,260 --> 00:32:26,260
It's similar in this sense.

488
00:32:26,260 --> 00:32:32,260
When there is non-symmetry in a market, it gives us a little bit of a lean in one direction or the other.

489
00:32:32,260 --> 00:32:35,260
And that's why this principle is crucial to understand.

490
00:32:35,260 --> 00:32:44,260
So, it's advantageous and poor odds opportunities to go against when there is this natural tendency in the market.

491
00:32:44,260 --> 00:32:45,860
So, examples of non-symmetry would

492
00:32:45,860 --> 00:32:48,100
be a trending market or a B

493
00:32:48,100 --> 00:32:52,260
shape or a P shape, sorry, a B or a P shape distribution.

494
00:32:52,260 --> 00:32:57,260
Now, we will talk a little bit about the B shape and the P shape distributions

495
00:32:57,260 --> 00:33:02,260
as we go further along. Now, in a non-symmetrical skew,

496
00:33:02,260 --> 00:33:06,260
there is an initiative that has been taken by either buyers or sellers.

497
00:33:06,260 --> 00:33:12,260
So, note, it starts off with an initiative or initiative taken by influential players.

498
00:33:12,260 --> 00:33:15,738
The change in the price that has occurred,

499
00:33:15,738 --> 00:33:18,346
it forces other timeframe participants to

500
00:33:18,346 --> 00:33:22,259
respond and hop onto the trend or counter it.

501
00:33:22,260 --> 00:33:31,260
This countering eventually causes an imbalance in positions and prices and it eventually results in a seemingly aggressive price response.

502
00:33:31,260 --> 00:33:33,567
So, note, when we have

503
00:33:33,567 --> 00:33:36,797
this imbalance, we get the initiative, that

504
00:33:36,797 --> 00:33:39,566
change in price, that aggressive change

505
00:33:39,566 --> 00:33:43,258
in price brings other timeframe participants into play.

506
00:33:43,260 --> 00:33:48,260
And that is what creates the volume and volatility and the opportunity for us.

507
00:33:48,260 --> 00:33:54,700
Let's continue our discussion now of non-symmetrical and symmetrical profiles.

508
00:33:54,700 --> 00:33:59,700
As you can see in the example provided, we have a very balanced distribution.

509
00:33:59,700 --> 00:34:08,699
Now, remember what we said, a normal distribution is where buyers initiate and are responsive at lows of the range

510
00:34:08,699 --> 00:34:13,699
and sellers initiate and are responsive at the high of the range.

511
00:34:13,699 --> 00:34:16,556
So, by this very token, when a market

512
00:34:16,556 --> 00:34:19,056
is distributing, we can see that the

513
00:34:19,056 --> 00:34:23,698
odds are very evenly split as to whether we go up or down.

514
00:34:23,699 --> 00:34:25,859
Now, note, one of the key

515
00:34:25,859 --> 00:34:28,019
things to point out when a

516
00:34:28,019 --> 00:34:30,899
market is very heavily balanced is that playing

517
00:34:30,899 --> 00:34:32,699
for momentum and being a

518
00:34:32,699 --> 00:34:41,699
very aggressive, looking for momentum breakouts to the upside and to the downside can be quite a risky play when the markets are in balance.

519
00:34:41,699 --> 00:34:49,699
We then have out-of-balance type profiles. These will be profiles or markets that are trending.

520
00:34:49,699 --> 00:34:52,339
Now, note, trending is when all

521
00:34:52,339 --> 00:34:54,979
five timeframes or all five timeframe

522
00:34:54,979 --> 00:35:00,259
participants are trading in the same direction, as you'll remember from Module 1.

523
00:35:00,700 --> 00:35:03,700
There's very little responsive type trading.

524
00:35:03,700 --> 00:35:14,700
Therefore, all timeframes are in agreement of direction and, hence, any responsive action taken is likely to eventually be overwhelmed

525
00:35:14,700 --> 00:35:16,700
as the initiative continues.

526
00:35:16,700 --> 00:35:21,700
We will talk a lot more about trending markets as we move further along.

527
00:35:21,700 --> 00:35:23,950
Note, then, another type of

528
00:35:23,950 --> 00:35:27,550
out-of-balance type shape is what we call

529
00:35:27,550 --> 00:35:31,150
a P shape, also known as short covering.

530
00:35:30,700 --> 00:35:36,700
Short covering is where the shorts cover their positions by buying out of those positions.

531
00:35:36,700 --> 00:35:43,700
Now, note, initiative selling, so shorts, they run into strong responsive long timeframe buyers.

532
00:35:43,700 --> 00:35:48,700
These buyers are responding to lower prices being advertised.

533
00:35:48,700 --> 00:35:51,300
The buyers then force prices higher

534
00:35:51,300 --> 00:35:54,766
and the shorts that are still holding their

535
00:35:54,766 --> 00:35:57,366
short positions will then buy out

536
00:35:57,366 --> 00:36:01,699
of those shorts and prices will continue to move higher.

537
00:36:01,700 --> 00:36:08,700
Those prices will continue to move up until we run into fresh responsive shorts.

538
00:36:08,700 --> 00:36:12,700
When that occurs, the market will then start to move sideways.

539
00:36:12,700 --> 00:36:20,700
The other type of out-of-balance type profile is a B shape or a long liquidation profile.

540
00:36:20,700 --> 00:36:22,700
In the B distribution,

541
00:36:22,700 --> 00:36:26,700
buyers this time, they run into aggressive initiative sellers.

542
00:36:26,700 --> 00:36:28,700
So we have longs in the market.

543
00:36:28,700 --> 00:36:34,700
They keep buying higher prices until they run into very strong responsive sellers.

544
00:36:34,700 --> 00:36:38,128
Those responsive sellers keep selling and eventually the

545
00:36:38,128 --> 00:36:41,128
buyers are forced to liquidate their longs

546
00:36:41,128 --> 00:36:43,699
by selling out of their position.

547
00:36:43,700 --> 00:36:55,700
This takes prices further lower until such a point that other longer timeframe buyers respond to relatively cheaper prices.

548
00:36:55,700 --> 00:36:59,700
This then stabilizes the market and we see further two-way trade.

549
00:36:59,700 --> 00:37:02,482
Those are just some of the examples and

550
00:37:02,482 --> 00:37:07,699
some of the shapes you can expect to see when talking about symmetry and non-symmetry.

551
00:37:07,700 --> 00:37:15,700
Let's take a look at some of the typical profiling examples you would see when talking about non-symmetrical profiles.

552
00:37:15,700 --> 00:37:19,700
As you can see, the first example is a non-symmetrical trend day.

553
00:37:19,700 --> 00:37:22,033
Now, what's important here is not too

554
00:37:22,033 --> 00:37:24,366
much about talking about trend or it's

555
00:37:24,366 --> 00:37:26,699
just understanding the basic shape of non-symmetry.

556
00:37:26,700 --> 00:37:34,700
Why non-symmetry is a particularly important principle for developing a very strong trade strategy.

557
00:37:34,700 --> 00:37:38,300
What you can see with regards to the trend is

558
00:37:38,300 --> 00:37:40,100
how for the entire day

559
00:37:40,100 --> 00:37:43,700
we've seen initiative being taken by sellers in this example.

560
00:37:43,700 --> 00:37:49,700
They keep selling and selling and selling and eventually they run into stronger responsive buying.

561
00:37:49,700 --> 00:37:52,123
That stronger responsive buying is what allows

562
00:37:52,123 --> 00:37:54,546
the market to almost just bounce a

563
00:37:54,546 --> 00:37:58,699
little bit and distribute and trade a little bit of two-way trade.

564
00:37:58,700 --> 00:38:01,033
Now, the important thing to note is

565
00:38:01,033 --> 00:38:02,699
that this non-symmetry or this

566
00:38:02,699 --> 00:38:05,699
lack of symmetry creates very good opportunity for us.

567
00:38:05,700 --> 00:38:12,700
So by that very token, non-symmetrical trend days are fantastic opportunities and you should be capitalizing on them.

568
00:38:12,700 --> 00:38:14,551
Not to worry though, we

569
00:38:14,551 --> 00:38:18,254
will spend a lot of time talking about trend and

570
00:38:18,254 --> 00:38:22,698
how you can develop strategies based purely on just simple non-symmetrical profiles.

571
00:38:22,700 --> 00:38:26,700
The second profile then is a very good example of short covering.

572
00:38:26,700 --> 00:38:29,700
As you can see with that K print, we can see initially

573
00:38:29,700 --> 00:38:35,700
the market opened a lot lower and ran into some very, very aggressive responsive buying.

574
00:38:35,700 --> 00:38:42,700
That buying drove prices up to such a point that the sellers were forced to liquidate their short positions.

575
00:38:42,700 --> 00:38:47,700
Eventually the market ran into fresh responsive selling.

576
00:38:47,700 --> 00:38:55,700
That fresh responsive selling managed to stop the flow of buying and we saw two-way trade ensue.

577
00:38:55,700 --> 00:38:59,774
Interestingly, what we saw was the sellers eventually overwhelmed those

578
00:38:59,774 --> 00:39:03,033
buyers and took the market all the way

579
00:39:03,033 --> 00:39:06,699
back to the low and closed quite aggressively lower.

580
00:39:06,700 --> 00:39:11,700
The third example is a B shape or long liquidation.

581
00:39:11,700 --> 00:39:14,900
Again, what we can see here is that

582
00:39:14,900 --> 00:39:18,100
the market opens, it tries to move higher,

583
00:39:18,100 --> 00:39:23,700
the buyers try to initiate higher prices before running into very aggressive responsive sellers.

584
00:39:23,700 --> 00:39:27,700
Those responsive sellers keep selling those prices and buyers are forced

585
00:39:27,700 --> 00:39:31,700
to liquidate their long positions by selling out.

586
00:39:31,700 --> 00:39:37,700
This takes prices down up to such a point that we run into new fresh responsive buyers.

587
00:39:37,700 --> 00:39:42,700
When that occurs, we then start to distribute and form two-way trade.

588
00:39:42,700 --> 00:39:48,700
It's so important you focus and understand these three basic non-symmetrical shapes

